            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Says which band each value falls in, given the boundaries between the bands: 0 for below the first boundary, 1 for between the first and the second, and so on up to the number of boundaries.\n\nIt is quantising with boundaries you choose. Use it when the bands mean something - 'still', 'moving', 'fast' at speeds that matter - rather than being equal slices of a range. Its output can go on into t.bincount to count how much time was spent in each band: a histogram of unequal, meaningful bins.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.bucketize\nNo arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.bucketize\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values, any shape. A single number works too. Every input is assigned and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send - but only once boundaries have arrived; until then nothing is sent.\n\nboundaries tensor in:\nThe boundaries, a 1-D list in INCREASING order (out of order gives wrong answers, with no error). They are kept until replaced, and converted to the type of the input - so with an integer input, boundaries like 2.5 are cut to 2.\n\nright (checkbox):\nWhich band a value exactly ON a boundary goes in. Off (the default): the band below - band i holds values above boundary i-1 up to and including boundary i. On: the band above.\n\nint32 indices (checkbox):\nSend the band numbers as int32 instead of int64.\n\nChanging either checkbox does not send by itself; the next input uses it. The metro in the demo re-sends, so the effect shows at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nbin count tensor out:\nThe band number of each value, whole numbers from 0 to the number of boundaries, the same shape as the input. (Despite the name, it is not a count.)\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe boundaries are 2 3 8, which make four bands: up to 2, above 2 up to 3, above 3 up to 8, above 8. The top strip is 0 to 10; the strip under it is each value's band. 0 1 2 are band 0, 3 is band 1, 4 to 8 band 2, 9 and 10 band 3 - the values sitting exactly on 2, 3 and 8 go to the band below. Tick right and they move up: 2 joins band 1, 3 and 8 band 2 and 3. Edit the boundaries message (they must increase) and click it.\n\nBelow, the slider sends single values from 0 to 10 into a second t.bucketize with the same boundaries, and the number shows the band it is in - the everyday use: which state is this reading in now?\n\nRELATED:\nt.bincount counts the band numbers. t.histc counts values in equal bins. t.round and t.clamp are simpler ways to quantise or limit values.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 975,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Says which band each value falls in, given the boundaries between the bands: 0 for below the first boundary, 1 for between the first and the second, and so on up to the number of boundaries.\n\nIt is quantising with boundaries you choose. Use it when the bands mean something - 'still', 'moving', 'fast' at speeds that matter - rather than being equal slices of a range. Its output can go on into t.bincount to count how much time was spent in each band: a histogram of unequal, meaningful bins.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.bucketize\nNo arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.bucketize\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values, any shape. A single number works too. Every input is assigned and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send - but only once boundaries have arrived; until then nothing is sent.\n\nboundaries tensor in:\nThe boundaries, a 1-D list in INCREASING order (out of order gives wrong answers, with no error). They are kept until replaced, and converted to the type of the input - so with an integer input, boundaries like 2.5 are cut to 2.\n\nright (checkbox):\nWhich band a value exactly ON a boundary goes in. Off (the default): the band below - band i holds values above boundary i-1 up to and including boundary i. On: the band above.\n\nint32 indices (checkbox):\nSend the band numbers as int32 instead of int64.\n\nChanging either checkbox does not send by itself; the next input uses it. The metro in the demo re-sends, so the effect shows at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nbin count tensor out:\nThe band number of each value, whole numbers from 0 to the number of boundaries, the same shape as the input. (Despite the name, it is not a count.)\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe boundaries are 2 3 8, which make four bands: up to 2, above 2 up to 3, above 3 up to 8, above 8. The top strip is 0 to 10; the strip under it is each value's band. 0 1 2 are band 0, 3 is band 1, 4 to 8 band 2, 9 and 10 band 3 - the values sitting exactly on 2, 3 and 8 go to the band below. Tick right and they move up: 2 joins band 1, 3 and 8 band 2 and 3. Edit the boundaries message (they must increase) and click it.\n\nBelow, the slider sends single values from 0 to 10 into a second t.bucketize with the same boundaries, and the number shows the band it is in - the everyday use: which state is this reading in now?\n\nRELATED:\nt.bincount counts the band numbers. t.histc counts values in equal bins. t.round and t.clamp are simpler ways to quantise or limit values."
        },
